"""
EFS - Monthly Audit / Capacity & Performance Report (MULTI-ACCOUNT)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Runs in a CENTRAL account. Triggered by EventBridge on the 1st of each
month. For each member account in MEMBER_ACCOUNT_IDS, assumes a
cross-account role, pulls every EFS file system's daily (day 1 -> last
day) storage/throughput data for the PREVIOUS month, and combines
everything into ONE HTML report with an Account column, uploads it to
S3 in the central account, signs a 7-day presigned URL, and emails it
via SNS.

ENV VARS REQUIRED:
  REPORT_BUCKET          - S3 bucket (in the central account) to store the report
  SNS_TOPIC_ARN           - SNS topic (in the central account) to notify
  KMS_KEY_ID              - CMK ARN/ID if REPORT_BUCKET enforces SSE-KMS
  MEMBER_ACCOUNT_IDS      - comma-separated list of account IDs to
                             report on, e.g. "111111111111,222222222222,
                             333333333333,444444444444,555555555555,
                             666666666666". Include the central account's
                             own ID here too if it also hosts EFS file
                             systems you want reported - the role is
                             assumed the same way in every account,
                             including the central one, for one
                             consistent code path.
  CROSS_ACCOUNT_ROLE_NAME - name of the IAM role (must exist in every
                             member account) this Lambda assumes,
                             default "EFSMonthlyReportRole"
  ACCOUNT_NAMES            - optional JSON object mapping account ID to
                             a friendly name, e.g.
                             '{"111111111111":"Prod","222222222222":"Dev"}'
                             Accounts not listed show their ID only.
  PRESIGNED_URL_EXPIRY_SECONDS - optional, default "900" (15 minutes).
                             The link is signed with THIS Lambda's own
                             execution role - see the note below on why
                             that means keeping this short.
  WARN_THRESHOLD_PCT      - optional, default "75"
  BAD_THRESHOLD_PCT       - optional, default "90"

PRESIGNED URL - ROLE-BASED, SHORT-LIVED (no Secrets Manager needed):
  Unlike the FSx report (which uses a long-term IAM user's keys stored
  in Secrets Manager to sign a guaranteed 7-day link), this report signs
  its presigned URL with the Lambda's OWN execution role credentials.
  That's simpler to set up - no secret to create/rotate in the central
  account - but the trade-off is real: Lambda periodically rotates its
  execution role's underlying temporary credentials on a schedule AWS
  doesn't publish a fixed guarantee for. Once that happens, a URL signed
  with the old credentials stops working immediately, regardless of the
  ExpiresIn value. PRESIGNED_URL_EXPIRY_SECONDS defaults to 900 (15
  minutes), which is safe in virtually all cases - don't push this much
  past an hour. If you need a guaranteed multi-day link for EFS the way
  FSx has, set up the same Secrets Manager signing-user pattern here
  too (see FSx's SIGNING_SECRET_ID / generate_presigned_url in
  report_common.py) - just ask and I'll wire it in.

CROSS-ACCOUNT SETUP REQUIRED (see README + the two new IAM JSON files):
  1. In EACH member account, create a role named CROSS_ACCOUNT_ROLE_NAME
     with a trust policy that trusts this Lambda's execution role
     (efs-cross-account-role-trust-policy.json), and a permissions
     policy granting elasticfilesystem:Describe* / cloudwatch:GetMetricData
     (efs-cross-account-role-permissions-policy.json).
  2. In the CENTRAL account, this Lambda's own execution role needs
     sts:AssumeRole on all 6 role ARNs (iam-policy-efs-report.json has
     been updated accordingly - it no longer needs direct EFS/CloudWatch
     permissions since all data collection now happens via the assumed
     role in each member account).

IF AN ACCOUNT FAILS (bad role, no permissions, account suspended, etc.):
  That account is SKIPPED, not silently dropped - the report includes an
  "Account Collection Status" table showing every account and whether it
  succeeded, so failures are visible for audit rather than just missing.

NOTES ON METRICS:
  StorageBytes is published by EFS with a "StorageClass" dimension of
  Total / Standard / IA (and Archive, if enabled), once per day.
  Throughput/connections metrics (PermittedThroughput, MeteredIOBytes,
  ClientConnections, BurstCreditBalance, PercentIOLimit) are pulled as
  daily AVERAGE for trending and daily MAXIMUM for burst-credit / peak
  connection callouts.
"""

